failure.body:
**Description**

The non default VRF "Vrf1" fails to be deleted during "config vrf del Vrf1" execution in vrf/test_vrf.py::TestVrfDeletion due to references still existing.

**Steps to reproduce the issue:**
Deploy t0 topology
Run vrf/test_vrf.py::TestVrfDeletion

**Describe the results you received:**
The Vrf1 should be removed from config DB as well as from the ASIC DB.

**Describe the results you expected:**
The Vrf1 is removed from the config Db only and still remains in the ASIC DB.

**Additional information you deem important (e.g. issue happens only occasionally):**

The sonic-buildimage commit: **dbc67184083fa91fe13c65cd5336f9455ac61a36**

- After some investigation done I found that the Vrf1 fails to remove as it's still referenced by PortChannel0002 RIF which in turn is referenced by a Neighbor fc00::76 which fails to be removed as well due to references still existing. This happens with a 6K routes configured during T0 deployment with default parameters.

- If the routes number is lowered to ~1K the PortChannel002 RIF is removed as well as the neighbor. The Vrf1 is still referenced though by the route <IP>/21 - the Vrf1 ref_count for this route is incremented in  https://github.com/Azure/sonic-swss/blob/master/orchagent/routeorch.cpp#L1246 but looks to be not decremented on removal. If add Vrf decrease to this line: https://github.com/Azure/sonic-swss/blob/master/orchagent/routeorch.cpp#L1712 the ref count goes below zero and orchagent gets crashed. If I comment out ref count increase in routeorch.cpp#L1246 the ref count looks good and Vrf gets removed but the orchagent still gets crashed due to meta_generic_validation_remove() check fail as the Vrf1 object is still referenced by default (<IP>/0, ::/0) routes created (couldn't find place were exactly) for it.

- Regarding the Neighbor referenced on removal with 6K routes - looks like the issue happens in addRoutePost() in this place:

```
        /* Increase the ref_count for the next hop (group) entry */
        increaseNextHopRefCount(nextHops);

        decreaseNextHopRefCount(it_route->second);
```

Recently this code was changed, so there is the possibility that it was fixed. But what originally caused was that the NH fc00::76 ref_count was increased whereas the NH Group with this nexthop - decreased. At the same time the removeRoutePost() was called which removed the routes with NHG but the ref count of the nexthop fc00::76 had been already incremented so when all the routes were removed ref_count was not zero. With the debug patch below the problem looks even more obvious as the debug logs add more latency to this whole addRoutePost(), removeRoutePost() process:

**debug_patch**
